The code statements that lead to the bug are:

```c
guest_fpu->fpstate->xfd = xfd;
if (guest_fpu->fpstate->in_use)
    xfd_update_state(guest_fpu->fpstate);
```
```c
if (ustate->xsave.header.xfeatures & ~xcr0)
    return -EINVAL;
/* No check against kstate->xfd here */
```
Reason:  
In `fpu_update_guest_xfd`, the original code updates the guest XFD but does not clear any bits in `XSTATE_BV` of the saved FPU state. If the guest FPU state is not currently loaded (e.g., due to an interrupt or preemption), the stale `XSTATE_BV` bits for features disabled by the new XFD remain set. A subsequent `XRSTOR` with the guest XFD will then try to load a disabled state component, causing a `#NM` exception and kernel panic. Similarly, in `fpu_copy_uabi_to_guest_fpstate`, the original code only validates `xfeatures` against `xcr0`, but not against the guest’s current `xfd`. This allows userspace to set `XSTATE_BV` bits for XFD-disabled features via `KVM_SET_XSAVE`, leading to the same `#NM` on later load.

The code statements that fix the bug are:

```c
if (xfd && test_thread_flag(TIF_NEED_FPU_LOAD))
    fpstate->regs.xsave.header.xfeatures &= ~xfd;
```
```c
if (WARN_ON_ONCE(ustate->xsave.header.xfeatures & kstate->xfd))
    return -EINVAL;
```
Reason:  
In `fpu_update_guest_xfd`, the new check clears any bits in the saved `XSTATE_BV` that are disabled by the new `xfd` when the guest state is not resident in hardware (indicated by `TIF_NEED_FPU_LOAD`). This ensures that a later `XRSTOR` will not attempt to load disabled state. In `fpu_copy_uabi_to_guest_fpstate`, the new check rejects any user-supplied `XSTATE_BV` bits that are disabled by the guest `xfd`. This prevents the kernel from ever storing an inconsistent `XSTATE_BV` where a disabled feature’s bit is set. Together, these changes guarantee that `XSTATE_BV[i]=0` whenever `XFD[i]=1`, preventing the `#NM` exception.

